<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="L-tyrosine biosynthesis II" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-3461" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; The three aromatic amino acids, |FRAME: TRP|, |FRAME: TYR|, and |FRAME: PHE|, derived from the common precursor |FRAME: CHORISMATE|, are constituents of proteins, and precursors of many plant secondary metabolites. Two steps are required for conversion of |FRAME: PREPHENATE| to |FRAME: TYR|: a dehydrogenation/decarboxylation and a transamination. Two possible routes for this conversion are known. In the route described in |FRAME: TYRSYN| the decarboxylation occurs first, resulting in the intermediate |FRAME: P-HYDROXY-PHENYLPYRUVATE|, followed by a transamination to |FRAME: TYR|. In the second route, described here, transamination occurs first, resulting in formation of |FRAME: CPD-659|, which is subsequently decarboxylated to |FRAME: TYR|. While the first route has been confirmed in many microorganisms, including E.coli and yeast, some other bacteria, including cyanobacteria, as well as many plants, employ the arogenate route |CITS: [4206476][3298985]|. The two routes have been reported to co-exist in many microorganisms. In addition, the |FRAME: P-HYDROXY-PHENYLPYRUVATE| route cannot be completely ruled out for plants based on these facts: 1) early studies detected prephenate dehydrogenase activity in mung bean shoots |CITS: [4379953]|, 2) the Arabidopsis arogenate dehydrogenase TyrAAT2 presents a weak prephenate dehydrogenase activity |CITS: [12354106]|, even though the high Km for prephenate and low specific activity of prephenate dehydrogenase reaction indicates the reaction is unlikely physiologically significant. Two types of arogenate dehydrogenase are known, that utilze either NADP+ or NAD+. These two types are described by the pathways |FRAME: PWY-3461| and |FRAME: PWY-6120|, respectively. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; The two key reactions in this pathway are catalyzed by the enzymes |FRAME: MONOMER-8101| and |FRAME: AT5G34930-MONOMER|, respectively. Arogenate is the branch-point and the immediate precursor for the synthesis of both tyrosine and phenylalanine. Arogenate dehydrogenase is the key enzyme channeling arogenate towards tyrosine synthesis. Its activity is strongly feedback inhibited by tyrosine. The Arabidopsis |FRAME: AT5G34930-MONOMER| |CITS:[12354106]|, as well as the enzyme from the cyanobacterium |FRAME: TAX-1148| |CITS: [15171683]|, are strictly NADP-dependent (see |FRAME: PWY-3461|). The enzymes from maize |CITS:[Byng1981]| and the actinobacterium |FRAME: TAX-1866| |CITS:[2590341]| are strictly NAD-dependent.</Description>
